    {
      "section": "legal.policy-header",
      "variant": "default",
      "content": {
        "title": "Supply chain statement",
        "updated": "Last updated: 1 October 2026, version 3"
      },
      "motion": {
        "pattern": "static",
        "reducedMotionFallback": "none-needed"
      }
    },
    {
      "section": "legal.policy-body",
      "variant": "default",
      "content": {
        "blocks": [
          {
            "kind": "h2",
            "text": "About this page"
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "*Placeholder text for a fictional company. This page is not a real policy and has no legal effect.* It shows how a short statement about fair labour in a supply chain might be laid out for a software company like Lattice, with the headings and lists a reader would expect to find."
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "**Scope of the statement.** In this example, the statement covers the fictional Lattice group and every subsidiary listed in its annual report for the twelve months ending in September. It describes the steps the company says it takes to make sure that people working for it, and for the businesses it buys from, are treated fairly, paid properly and free to leave their jobs. A real statement would be approved by the board, signed by a director and published within six months of the year end."
          },
          {
            "kind": "h2",
            "text": "Who we are"
          },
          {
            "kind": "h3",
            "text": "Our business"
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "Lattice, in this placeholder, builds software that helps data teams model, test and share information. Its employees are mostly engineers, designers, support staff and sales people who work from offices in a handful of cities or from home. The company does not manufacture physical goods, run warehouses or operate retail stores, so most of its exposure to labour risk sits with suppliers rather than within its own workforce."
          },
          {
            "kind": "h3",
            "text": "Our supply chain"
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "The fictional company buys cloud hosting, office space and services, laptops and other hardware, marketing services, travel, catering for events and branded merchandise. A small number of these categories, especially hardware and merchandise, rely on manufacturing in regions where workers can be more vulnerable, and those categories receive the closest attention in the steps described below."
          },
          {
            "kind": "h2",
            "text": "Policies that set our expectations for suppliers"
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "In this placeholder, Lattice puts its expectations into the purchase contract itself rather than into a separate document. Every order above a modest value carries four fair-work clauses: wages are paid in full, on time and into an account the worker controls; overtime is voluntary and recorded; nobody pays a broker or agency to be hired; and the buyer may visit any site that makes goods for it with two weeks of notice. A fifth clause asks the seller to place the same four promises in its own purchase contracts. Alongside the contracts, the fictional company funds a multilingual phone and text line, run by an independent charity, that anyone in its supply chain can use to report a problem without giving their name; the charity passes on reports in summary and never shares the identity of a caller. Internally, a single page in the employee handbook explains who owns each clause, how a breach is escalated and how the finance team holds payment while a serious report is checked. The legal team reviews the clauses with an outside adviser each spring and records what changed and why."
          },
          {
            "kind": "h2",
            "text": "How we assess risk and check that our suppliers meet the standards we set"
          },
          {
            "kind": "h3",
            "text": "Mapping the supply chain"
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "Each year the procurement team, in this example, exports a list of every supplier paid more than a set amount and groups them by category and country. Categories are scored for inherent risk using publicly available indices and the team’s own judgement, which produces a short list of suppliers that deserve a closer look."
          },
          {
            "kind": "h3",
            "text": "Questions we ask"
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "Suppliers on the short list receive a questionnaire about how they recruit, pay and house workers, whether they use agencies, and how they check their own suppliers. Answers are reviewed by someone outside the team that manages the relationship."
          },
          {
            "kind": "h3",
            "text": "Signals that prompt a closer look"
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "The team watches for a small set of warning signs that would trigger a deeper review or an independent audit:"
          },
          {
            "kind": "ul",
            "items": [
              {
                "text": "Unusually low prices"
              },
              {
                "text": "Reluctance to share factory details"
              },
              {
                "text": "Heavy use of labour agencies"
              },
              {
                "text": "Recent news reports about the region or sector"
              },
              {
                "text": "Missing or late answers to questions"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "kind": "h3",
            "text": "When something is wrong"
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "If a review finds a problem, the placeholder policy is to work with the supplier on a written plan with clear dates rather than to walk away immediately, because ending a contract can leave workers worse off. Where a supplier refuses to engage, or where workers are in immediate danger, the relationship ends and the matter is reported to the relevant authorities."
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "Progress on each plan is reviewed every quarter by the procurement lead and summarised for the board once a year, alongside the number of suppliers reviewed and the number of plans opened and closed."
          },
          {
            "kind": "h2",
            "text": "Training our employees"
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "Everyone in the fictional company completes a short course on recognising signs of forced labour and on how to raise a concern, and people who buy goods or services on the company’s behalf take a longer course every year. Completion rates are tracked by the people team and reported alongside the statement, together with a short summary of any questions or concerns that the training prompted."
          },
          {
            "kind": "h2",
            "text": "What we measured this year and what we plan to do next"
          },
          {
            "kind": "h3",
            "text": "Measures of progress"
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "A real statement would report numbers here. In this placeholder version, the company tracks the share of new contracts carrying the fair-work clauses, the number of short-listed suppliers who returned the questionnaire, the number of independent audits carried out and the number of concerns raised through the whistleblowing line."
          },
          {
            "kind": "ul",
            "items": [
              {
                "text": "Fair-work clauses appeared in every purchase contract signed during the year, and the eleven sellers who asked to amend them were given a short call with the legal team, after which nine signed the standard wording and two agreed adjusted notice periods for site visits."
              },
              {
                "text": "Most short-listed suppliers returned the questionnaire within the agreed time, and the remainder were contacted directly by the procurement lead to understand the delay and agree a new date."
              },
              {
                "text": "Two independent audits were carried out at merchandise suppliers, and both led to short improvement plans covering working hours and the way overtime was recorded and paid to temporary staff."
              },
              {
                "text": "No concerns about forced labour were raised through the whistleblowing line, although several general questions about supplier standards were answered and added to the internal frequently asked questions page."
              },
              {
                "text": "Completion of the annual training course reached nearly every eligible employee, and the longer course for people who buy goods or services reached all of them before the end of the reporting period."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "kind": "h3",
            "text": "Plans for the coming year"
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "For the next reporting period, the fictional company plans to extend the questionnaire to a wider group of suppliers, add clearer guidance for event organisers and publish a short summary of audit findings for employees."
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "It also intends to review how risk scores are calculated, so that new suppliers in high-risk categories are checked before their first order rather than at the next annual review."
          },
          {
            "kind": "ul",
            "items": [
              {
                "text": "Bring every seller of branded merchandise and event services into the questionnaire process, and ask each one to confirm the countries in which their own suppliers make the products they sell to us."
              },
              {
